bench:qa:24.answer_text:
Before placing a high‑risk AI system on the EU market, an importer must, in essence, verify that the system is compliant and properly documented and labelled. This includes checking that:
- the provider has carried out the applicable conformity assessment and drawn up the required EU declaration of conformity;
- the system bears the required CE marking and is accompanied by the required instructions for use and information;
- the provider and the system can be identified (e.g., required identification/traceability details), and the importer’s own identification details are indicated as required;
- where relevant, the importer has ensured appropriate storage/transport conditions so the system’s compliance is not compromised.

If the importer has reason to believe the system is not in conformity, it should not place it on the market until it has been brought into conformity and should take appropriate steps (including informing the provider and, where applicable, competent authorities).

bench:qa:25.answer_text:
Before making a high‑risk AI system available on the market, a distributor must verify that:
- the system bears the required CE marking;
- it is accompanied by the required EU declaration of conformity and the required instructions for use;
- the provider and (where applicable) the importer have complied with their respective obligations (including that the importer has indicated the required identification/contact details).
If the distributor has reason to consider the system is not in conformity, it must not make it available until it has been brought into conformity, and must take appropriate steps (including informing the provider/importer and, where relevant, competent authorities).

bench:qa:29.answer_text:
Under Article 28, the notifying authority is the Member State body responsible for setting up and carrying out the national notification system for conformity assessment bodies. Its role includes:
- assessing, designating and notifying conformity assessment bodies (and any changes affecting their notification) to the Commission and other Member States;
- monitoring and supervising notified bodies on an ongoing basis to ensure they continue to meet the requirements for notification, including handling updates, restrictions, suspensions or withdrawals of notifications where needed;
- ensuring the notifying authority itself is organised and operates so as to be objective, impartial and free from conflicts of interest, with competent staff and appropriate procedures.

In short, it is the gatekeeper and supervisor for which conformity assessment bodies can act as “notified bodies” under the AI Act in that Member State.

bench:qa:30.answer_text:
When applying to be notified as a conformity‑assessment body under the EU AI Act, the applicant must submit an application to the notifying authority that includes:

- A description of the conformity‑assessment activities it intends to carry out;
- The conformity‑assessment module(s) it will apply;
- For each type of AI system it seeks to assess, the AI system(s) concerned; and
- An accreditation certificate issued by a national accreditation body (under Regulation (EC) No 765/2008), or—if it is not accredited—other documentary evidence demonstrating that it meets the requirements applicable to conformity‑assessment bodies.

If the application is based on documentary evidence rather than an accreditation certificate, the notifying authority must provide the Commission and the other Member States with that evidence.

bench:qa:54.answer_text:
AI regulatory sandboxes are controlled frameworks set up by public authorities (typically national competent authorities) that let providers and other participants develop, train, test and validate AI systems in real-world or close-to-real-world conditions for a limited time, under regulatory oversight and with agreed safeguards.

In the EU AI Act context, their purpose is to:
- enable experimentation and learning while ensuring a high level of protection of health, safety and fundamental rights;
- support compliance by helping participants understand and meet the Act’s requirements (especially for high-risk AI);
- facilitate innovation, including for SMEs and start-ups, by providing guidance, supervision and a structured testing environment.

Sandboxes do not waive the AI Act’s obligations; they are a supervised way to test and iterate before (or while) placing an AI system on the market or putting it into service, subject to conditions set by the authority (e.g., risk-mitigation measures, monitoring, documentation, and limits on scope/duration).

bench:qa:96.answer_text:
Under the EU AI Act, providers of high-risk AI systems must establish, implement, document and maintain a quality‑management system (QMS) as part of their compliance obligations. The QMS must be proportionate to the nature of the provider and the type of high‑risk AI system, and it must cover the provider’s organisational structure, responsibilities and procedures needed to ensure ongoing conformity.

In particular, the QMS must include documented policies and procedures for (at least):
- compliance strategy and regulatory compliance processes (including how conformity will be ensured and demonstrated);
- design, development and testing controls for the high‑risk AI system;
- data management practices (including data governance relevant to training/validation/testing where applicable);
- technical documentation and record‑keeping processes;
- risk management and quality control (including verification/validation and change control);
- post‑market monitoring, incident handling and corrective actions (including reporting of serious incidents and malfunctioning as required);
- management of updates/modifications and configuration management;
- supplier/third‑party and component control where relevant (including outsourced processes);
- internal audits and continuous improvement.

Providers must keep the QMS up to date throughout the lifecycle of the high‑risk AI system and ensure it is effectively applied in practice (not only documented).

bench:qa:14.answer_text:
Under Article 10, where high‑risk AI systems use training techniques, they must be developed using training, validation and testing data sets that meet the Article 10(2)–(5) quality criteria. In particular, Article 10(3) requires those data sets to be:
- relevant;
- sufficiently representative;
- to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete for the system’s intended purpose; and
- to have appropriate statistical properties, including (where applicable) for the persons or groups of persons the system is intended to be used on.
These characteristics can be satisfied by each individual data set or by a combination of data sets.

bench:qa:25.answer_text:
Before making a high-risk AI system available on the market, distributors must verify that:
- the system bears the required CE marking;
- it is accompanied by a copy of the EU declaration of conformity (Article 47);
- it is accompanied by instructions for use; and
- the provider and (as applicable) the importer have complied with their respective obligations under Article 16(b) and (c) and Article 23(3).

bench:qa:30.answer_text:
When applying for notification, a conformity‑assessment body’s application must be accompanied by:
- a description of its conformity‑assessment activities;
- the conformity‑assessment module(s) and the types of AI systems for which it claims competence;
- an accreditation certificate (where one exists) issued by a national accreditation body attesting it fulfils the requirements in Article 31; and
- any valid document related to existing designations of the applicant under other Union harmonisation legislation (to be added).

bench:qa:49.answer_text:
Under the AI Act, references to the “AI Office” are to be understood as references to the European Commission.

The AI Office also has specific implementation-support roles, including:
- encouraging and facilitating Union-level codes of practice to support effective implementation of obligations on detecting and labelling artificially generated or manipulated content;
- encouraging and facilitating the review and adaptation of codes of practice (notably in light of emerging standards) and assisting in the assessment of available standards;
- developing a template questionnaire (including via an automated tool) to help deployers comply with their obligations in a simplified manner;
- developing and recommending voluntary model contract terms for certain high-risk AI supply-chain relationships, while taking into account sector- or use-case-specific contractual requirements.

In addition, in the general-purpose AI model regime, authorised representatives must be able to provide their mandate to the AI Office on request, keep technical documentation at the AI Office’s disposal, provide information/documentation to the AI Office upon reasoned request to demonstrate compliance, and cooperate with the AI Office in actions it takes relating to the model.

bench:qa:74.answer_text:
For a high‑risk AI system, the provider’s instructions for use must include at least:

- The provider’s identity and contact details and, where applicable, those of its authorised representative.
- The system’s characteristics, capabilities and performance limitations, including:
  - its intended purpose;
  - the level of accuracy (including metrics), robustness and cybersecurity it was tested/validated against, and known/foreseeable circumstances that may affect expected levels;
  - any known/foreseeable circumstances (including reasonably foreseeable misuse) that may lead to risks to health, safety or fundamental rights;
  - where applicable, the system’s technical capabilities/characteristics to provide information relevant to explain its output;
  - when appropriate, performance regarding specific persons or groups on which it is intended to be used;
  - when appropriate, specifications for input data and/or other relevant information about the training/validation/testing datasets used, taking into account intended purpose;
  - where applicable, information enabling deployers to interpret the output and use it appropriately.
- Any pre-determined changes to the system and its performance identified by the provider at the time of the initial conformity assessment (if any).
- The human oversight measures (including technical measures facilitating deployers’ interpretation of outputs).
- The computational and hardware resources needed, expected lifetime, and necessary maintenance/care measures (including frequency) to ensure proper functioning, including software updates.
- Where relevant, a description of the logging mechanisms that allow deployers to properly collect, store and interpret logs in accordance with Article 12.

bench:qa:14.answer_text:
Under Article 10, high‑risk AI systems that use training techniques must be developed using training, validation and testing data sets that meet the Article 10(2)–(5) quality criteria. In particular, those data sets must be:
- relevant;
- sufficiently representative;
- to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete in view of the intended purpose; and
- have appropriate statistical properties (including, where applicable, regarding the persons or groups of persons the system is intended to be used in relation to).

bench:qa:20.answer_text:
Under the EU AI Act, the purpose of human oversight for high‑risk AI systems is to ensure that the system is provided and used in a way that enables the natural persons assigned oversight to:

- understand the system’s relevant capacities and limitations and monitor its operation, including to detect and address anomalies, dysfunctions and unexpected performance;
- remain aware of and mitigate automation bias (the tendency to automatically rely or over‑rely on the system’s output);
- correctly interpret the system’s output;
- decide, in a given situation, not to use the system or to disregard, override or reverse its output; and
- intervene in or interrupt the system’s operation (e.g., via a stop button) so it can be brought to a halt in a safe state.

The Act also links human oversight measures to facilitating deployers’ interpretation of high‑risk AI outputs via technical measures described in the instructions for use.

bench:qa:24.answer_text:
Before placing a high‑risk AI system on the market, importers shall ensure the system is in conformity with the Regulation by verifying that:
- the relevant conformity assessment procedure (Article 43) has been carried out by the provider;
- the provider has drawn up the technical documentation in accordance with Article 11 and Annex IV;
- the system bears the required CE marking and is accompanied by the EU declaration of conformity (Article 47) and instructions for use; and
- the provider has appointed an authorised representative in accordance with Article 22(1).

